## Overview of the management of chronic kidney disease in adults

- **Approach to general Mx of CKD:**
  - <u>Identification and Mx of reversible causes</u> - consider pre-renal, renal, and post-renal causes
  - <u>Prevention or slowing down progression of kidney disease</u> - Tx of underlying cause, BP control, proteinuria control
  - <u>Mx of complications of CKD</u> - dyslipidaemia and increased cardiovascular risk, renal anaemia, fluid overload, electrlyte and acid-base disturbances, mineral bone disease etc.
  - <u>Renal dosing of cocurrent medications</u> - e.g. use of metformin, anticoagulants other any other drugs
  - <u>Identification and preparation of patients anticipated to require RRT</u> - performed by a nephrologist and multidisciplinary team typically when ESRD is predicted to ensue within 12 mo
- **Identification and Mx of reversible causes** - consider acute on chronic kidney disease if there is a sudden recent decrease in eGFR:
  - **Pre-renal causes:**
    - <u>Hypovolaemia</u> - e.g. bleeding, N/V, over-diuresis
    - <u>Hypotension</u> - e.g. cardiovascular (including pericardial) disease
    - <u>Altered glomerular haemodynamics</u> - e.g. NSAIDs, RAAS blockade
  - **Renal causes** - esp. iodinated contrast agents, NSAIDs, aminoglycosides ABx, or other nephrotixic drugs (also flare of primary disease activity)
  - **Post-renal causes** - e.g. any causes of bilateral hydronephrosis (detected on renal USG)
- **Approach to slowing rate of progression** - related to 1) activity of initial disease, and 2) secondary factors such as glomerular haemodynamics, and other factors:
  - **Tx of underlying cause** - control activity of the initial inciting disease
  - **Mx of secondary factors:**
    - <u>BP control</u> - best w/ RAAS blockade
    - <u>Albuminuria</u> - w/ RAAS blockade or SGLT2i (even in non-diabetic patients)
    - <u>Protein restriction</u> - may slow down progression of CKD
    - <u>Smoking cessation</u> - associated w/ rate of progression of CKD
- **BP control** - aggressive BP control typically indicated in CKD patients where most have underlying HTN (80-85%):
  - **Rationale** - patients often have HTN (80-85%) or underlying hypervolaemia which require Mx, furthermore:
    - BP lowering prevents progression to ESRD
    - BP lowering results in reduced cardiovascular events in CKD patients (N.B. MACE are leading cause of death in CKD patients)
    - BP lowering is a/w lower overall mortality
  - **Selection** - dependent on underlying CKD:
    - <u>Diabetic CKD</u> - RAAS blockade as 1st-line therapy
    - <u>Albuminuric non-diabetic CKD</u> - RAAS blockade as 1st-line therapy
    - <u>Non-albuminuric non-diabetic CKD</u> - escalation of anti-hypertensives similar as in general population (which often includes RAAS bockade)
  - **Clinical efficacy of BP control** - Systolic Pressure Intervention Trial (SPRINT):
    - <u>Progression to ESRD</u> - intensive vs standard BP lowering results in reduced risk of ESRD w/ albuminuric CKD, but not in non-albuminuric CKD
    - <u>Mortality</u> - intensive vs standard BP lowering results in reduced in overall mortality (irrespective of whether albuminuric)
  - **BP target** - aggressive target of at least \< 130/ 80 mmHg:
    - <u>Most CKD patients</u> - target \< 130/80 mmHg
    - <u>CKD w/ significant proteinuria</u> (PCR \> 100 mg/mmol) - target \< 125/75 mmHg
- **Proteinuria control** - good proteinuric control a/w slower rate of progression:
  - **Selection:**
    - <u>RAAS blockade</u> - ACEi, ARBs
    - <u>SGLT2i</u> - dapagliflozin, empagliflozin
  - **Clinical efficacy:**
    - <u>RAAS blockade</u> - RAAS blockade results in reduced risk of ESRD w/ albuminuric CKD, but not in non-albuminuric CKD, suggesting its role in controling proteinuria
    - <u>Dapagliflozin</u> - DAPA-CKD trial demonstrates reduced incidence of progression to ESRD in albuminuric CKD irrespective of presence of diabetes
    - <u>Empagliflozin</u> - EMPA-KIDNEY trial demonstrates reduced incidence of progression to ESRD in albuminuric CKD irrespective of presence of diabetes
  - **Albuminuria goal** - albuminuria \< 300 mg/d (corresponding to UACR 300 mg/g in most individuals)

